WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.991
Ransomware demonstrates how an enterprise cyber incident can become an operational crisis.

00:00:04.991 --> 00:00:09.882
An attacker does not need to reprogram a controller to stop production.

00:00:09.882 --> 00:00:13.143
Loss of identity services, virtualization, engineering workstations, scheduling,

00:00:13.143 --> 00:00:13.959
quality systems,

00:00:13.959 --> 00:00:18.442
or operator support applications may make continued operation unsafe or impractical.

00:00:18.442 --> 00:00:23.333
Organizations sometimes shut down proactively because they cannot confirm where the intrusion

00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:26.187
reached or whether process visibility remains trustworthy.

00:00:26.187 --> 00:00:29.855
That decision protects operations, but it also creates downtime.

00:00:29.855 --> 00:00:34.746
The distinction between business systems and control systems still matters for investigation,

00:00:34.746 --> 00:00:38.415
yet the recovery plan must account for their dependencies.

00:00:38.415 --> 00:00:41.676
OT recovery is more complex than restoring files.

00:00:41.676 --> 00:00:46.567
Teams may need to rebuild trusted workstations, validate controller logic and configurations,

00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:51.050
confirm network boundaries, reconcile physical process state, and sequence equipment startup.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.496
Vendors and engineers may be required.

00:00:53.496 --> 00:00:58.795
A recovered server is not proof that the operation is safe to resume.

00:00:58.795 --> 00:01:04.501
Segmentation reduces the ability of ransomware to spread from user networks into site operations.

00:01:04.501 --> 00:01:06.947
Controlled identity and administration limit privilege.

00:01:06.947 --> 00:01:10.208
Reliable backups should include controller programs, device configurations,

00:01:10.208 --> 00:01:13.876
HMI and SCADA applications, historians, servers, and essential documentation.

00:01:13.876 --> 00:01:16.729
Backups need protected storage and practiced restoration.

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:21.621
Response planning should define who can isolate networks, pause production, validate equipment,

00:01:21.621 --> 00:01:24.066
communicate with partners, and authorize restart.

00:01:24.066 --> 00:01:27.327
Monitor for activity crossing IT and OT boundaries,

00:01:27.327 --> 00:01:29.773
but interpret alerts with operational context.

00:01:29.773 --> 00:01:33.034
Resilience comes from architecture, preparation, and coordinated recovery,

00:01:33.034 --> 00:01:38.740
not from assuming that ransomware will remain on one side of an organizational chart.
